hard work and having a passion for what  you can do can take you a long way in  your career but for many women in the  hotel or restaurant field the odds of  them making it as far as their male  counterparts aren't as good but there is  an effort to change those odds and it's  happening in the dining room the  classroom and the kitchen  maybe i can do the new rainbow trout  stuffed and be great chef gina marinelli  is one of a handful of women recently  nominated for a james beard award one of  the highest honors a chef can receive  when i first started a female executive  chef that didn't exist it's still a  rarity less than seven percent of head  chefs and restaurant owners nationwide  are women seven percent marinelli worked  her way from dishwasher to owner with  this motto that's the thing i always  want people to see is the hardest  working chef not the hardest working  female chef women in the industry have  not been treated the same as men for a  very long time restaurateur jolene  menina realized getting a seat at the  table means working harder smarter and  together how you doing nicole so she  helped launch the women's hospitality  initiative to get more women in  leadership roles both in front and back  of the house to give them the tools give  them some support find mentors for them  you know to find realize you know what  they're capable of doing to that end a  16-week course is now offered at  specialty schools across the country  aptly named women in hospitality  leadership each week dr lisa kane  features a different speaker who shares  their journey we've had celebrity chefs  speak in our class  and they talk about the challenges  they've had so if they've been able to  overcome them you can too there are lots  of challenges when it comes to salary  and titles the castel project released  its 2022 industry report women are  slowly gaining ground and hotel company  leadership now holding one ceo or  president title for every 10 men at the  management director levels women are one  in three florida international  university tracks its chaplain school  graduates we can tell you that men are  earning 91  hundred dollars in hospitality jobs  where women are making seventy three  thousand five hundred i think that's  what the beauty in this class is is it  generates awareness and the more aware  we are of our shortcomings the more we  can work to correct them like many other  industries several things need to happen  increasing pay transparency and  expanding paid medical and family leave  are just a start to evening out the  playing  field now dr kane has been pleasantly  surprised that a number of men are  taking that college course as well  making them aware early in their career  about the title and the salary disparity  and hopefully they can be part of the  solution when it comes to hiring and  placing women in executive positions
 

